From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 11:18:02 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xen: complete virtio-pci ISR reads on the ioreq thread under
 the BQL

Interrupts on Raspberry Pi 4 are INTx (the GIC-400 has no ITS, so there
is no MSI), and the guest's INTx handler reads the virtio-pci ISR
register on every interrupt to learn the cause and lower the line. A
read must be answered, so unlike a notify write it cannot be
acknowledged early by the ioreq thread; through the FIFO it waits for
the main loop to reach the handler that drains it. With DomA decoding
1080p60 video that was about 160 ISR reads a second at 1.05 ms each on
average, i.e. about 17% of a vCPU stalled.

When the ioreq thread (QEMU_XEN_IOREQ_THREAD=1) sees a plain read of a
"virtio-pci-isr-" region, it tries to take the BQL with a new
bql_trylock() helper. If it gets the lock and the FIFO is empty, it
executes the read itself and responds at once. Only the ioreq thread
adds to the FIFO and the main loop's handler drains it under the BQL, so
with the BQL held and the FIFO empty every earlier ioreq has already run
and none can start, so the ioreqs keep the order the handler would have
used. A bottom half that an earlier ioreq scheduled on the iohandler
context may not have run yet; the read then sees at worst an interrupt
not raised yet, which that bottom half raises afterwards, as a KVM vCPU
reading the register concurrently would. The ISR read handler only
exchanges vdev->isr and lowers the INTx line, which is what a KVM vCPU
thread does for the same register under the same lock; it does not touch
the GL context the main thread owns. The response goes out without
xen_ioreq_check_shutdown(), which may run qemu_system_reset() and with
it device resets that belong on the main thread: with a shutdown or
reset pending the read takes the FIFO, and one requested while the read
runs inline is picked up by the next ioreq the main thread completes.

A trylock rather than bql_lock() is used because waiting for the BQL
would hold the ioreq thread for as long as the main thread runs (58% of
the time at 1080p60), and the other vCPU's notify writes, otherwise
acknowledged in about 0.3 ms, would wait as long. If the BQL is busy or
anything is queued, the read takes the FIFO as before, so this path is
never worse than without it.

bql_trylock() keeps the bql_locked() bookkeeping through
qemu_mutex_post_lock(), as bql_lock() does. The 30 s statistics line
gains the number of ISR reads completed inline. Without the ioreq thread
(the default) nothing changes.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [this layer's Xen device model; switched by an environment variable]
---
 hw/xen/xen-hvm-common.c         | 92 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 include/hw/xen/xen-hvm-common.h |  1 +
 include/qemu/main-loop.h        |  1 +
 system/cpus.c                   | 10 ++++
 4 files changed, 102 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/xen/xen-hvm-common.c b/hw/xen/xen-hvm-common.c
index e5c8f0856..343f290bb 100644
--- a/hw/xen/xen-hvm-common.c
+++ b/hw/xen/xen-hvm-common.c
@@ -690,6 +690,87 @@ static bool xen_ioreq_is_virtio_notify(const ioreq_t *r)
     return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Is this ioreq a plain read of a virtio-pci ISR register? (no BQL; RCU)
+ *
+ * WHY THESE ARE WORTH A SHORTCUT. Interrupts are INTx on this platform (GIC-400 has
+ * no ITS, so no MSI), and the guest's INTx handler reads ISR on every interrupt to
+ * learn the cause and lower the line. A read must be answered, so it cannot be
+ * acknowledged early like a notify; through the FIFO it waits for the main loop to
+ * reach the handler. RPi4 DomA at 1080p60: ~160 of them a second, 1.05 ms each on
+ * average (2026-10-03), i.e. ~17% of a vCPU stalled.
+ */
+static bool xen_ioreq_is_virtio_isr_read(const ioreq_t *r)
+{
+    MemoryRegionSection sec;
+    bool ret;
+
+    if (r->type != IOREQ_TYPE_COPY || r->dir != IOREQ_READ ||
+        r->data_is_ptr || r->count != 1) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    sec = memory_region_find(get_system_memory(), r->addr, r->size);
+    if (!sec.mr) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    ret = g_str_has_prefix(memory_region_name(sec.mr), "virtio-pci-isr-");
+    memory_region_unref(sec.mr);
+    return ret;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Complete an ISR read on the ioreq thread, under the BQL, if nothing is queued
+ * ahead of it. Returns false (and does nothing) otherwise.
+ *
+ * ORDER. Only the ioreq thread adds to the FIFO, and the handler drains it under the BQL.
+ * Once this thread holds the BQL and sees the FIFO empty, every earlier ioreq has run and
+ * none can start until the BQL is dropped: the ioreqs keep the handler's order. A bottom
+ * half an earlier ioreq scheduled may not have run yet; the read then at worst sees an
+ * interrupt not raised yet, which that bottom half raises. Queued ahead -> normal path.
+ *
+ * SAFETY. The ISR read handler (virtio_pci_isr_read) only exchanges vdev->isr and
+ * lowers the INTx line -- what a KVM vCPU thread does for the same register under
+ * the same lock. It does not touch the GL context the main thread owns.
+ * The response is sent without xen_ioreq_check_shutdown(): that may run
+ * qemu_system_reset(), whose device resets (virtio-gpu-gl among them) belong on
+ * the main thread. A pending shutdown or reset sends the read the normal way; one
+ * requested while the read runs here is picked up by the next ioreq the main
+ * thread completes.
+ *
+ * WHAT IT BUYS. The read no longer waits for the main loop to reach the handler that
+ * drains the FIFO.
+ *
+ * TRYLOCK, NOT bql_lock(). Waiting for the BQL here would hold the ioreq thread for
+ * as long as the main thread runs (58% of the time at 1080p60), and meanwhile the
+ * OTHER vCPU's notify writes -- acknowledged in ~0.3 ms otherwise -- would wait as
+ * long. If the BQL is busy, the read takes the FIFO as before: never worse than
+ * without this path.
+ */
+static bool xen_ioreq_isr_inline(XenIOState *state, ioreq_t *req, int vcpu)
+{
+    ioreq_t copy = *req;
+    bool empty;
+
+    if (!bql_trylock()) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    qemu_mutex_lock(&state->ioreq_lock);
+    empty = QSIMPLEQ_EMPTY(&state->ioreq_fifo);
+    qemu_mutex_unlock(&state->ioreq_lock);
+    if (!empty || qemu_shutdown_requested_get() || qemu_reset_requested_get()) {
+        bql_unlock();
+        return false;
+    }
+    handle_ioreq(state, &copy);
+    req->data = copy.data;
+    xen_wmb(); /* Update ioreq contents /then/ update state. */
+    req->state = STATE_IORESP_READY;
+    qemu_xen_evtchn_notify(state->xce_handle, state->ioreq_local_port[vcpu]);
+    bql_unlock();
+    state->ioreq_isr_inline++;
+    return true;
+}
+
 /*
  * One queued ioreq per call, on the main loop's iohandler context: the context
  * cpu_handle_ioreq() runs on without the thread, and one ioreq a call, as it
@@ -792,6 +873,11 @@ static void *xen_ioreq_thread(void *opaque)
             continue;
         }
 
+        if (xen_ioreq_is_virtio_isr_read(req) &&
+            xen_ioreq_isr_inline(state, req, i)) {
+            continue;
+        }
+
         it = g_new0(XenIoreqItem, 1);
         it->copy = *req;
         it->vcpu = i;
@@ -818,8 +904,10 @@ static void *xen_ioreq_thread(void *opaque)
         now = qemu_clock_get_ms(QEMU_CLOCK_REALTIME);
         if (now - last >= 30000) {
             info_report("xen ioreq thread: %" PRIu64 " notify writes acknowledged "
-                        "early, %" PRIu64 " ioreqs completed by the main loop",
-                        state->ioreq_early, state->ioreq_queued);
+                        "early, %" PRIu64 " ISR reads completed inline, %" PRIu64
+                        " ioreqs completed by the main loop",
+                        state->ioreq_early, state->ioreq_isr_inline,
+                        state->ioreq_queued);
             last = now;
         }
     }
diff --git a/include/hw/xen/xen-hvm-common.h b/include/hw/xen/xen-hvm-common.h
index 19ec88c73..c713c0356 100644
--- a/include/hw/xen/xen-hvm-common.h
+++ b/include/hw/xen/xen-hvm-common.h
@@ -90,6 +90,7 @@ typedef struct XenIOState {
     bool ioreq_thread_on;
     uint64_t ioreq_early;
     uint64_t ioreq_queued;
+    uint64_t ioreq_isr_inline;
 
     Notifier exit;
 } XenIOState;
diff --git a/include/qemu/main-loop.h b/include/qemu/main-loop.h
index 8c1241a2c..a8540179b 100644
--- a/include/qemu/main-loop.h
+++ b/include/qemu/main-loop.h
@@ -367,6 +367,7 @@ bool qemu_in_main_thread(void);
  */
 #define bql_lock() bql_lock_impl(__FILE__, __LINE__)
 void bql_lock_impl(const char *file, int line);
+bool bql_trylock(void);
 
 /**
  * bql_unlock: Unlock the Big QEMU Lock (BQL).
diff --git a/system/cpus.c b/system/cpus.c
index 3e0cd4766..fd4fd209c 100644
--- a/system/cpus.c
+++ b/system/cpus.c
@@ -580,6 +580,16 @@ void bql_lock_impl(const char *file, int line)
     bql_lock_fn(&bql, file, line);
 }
 
+/*
+ * bql_trylock: take the BQL only if it is free; true on success. The bookkeeping
+ * behind bql_locked() is done by qemu_mutex_post_lock(), as for bql_lock().
+ */
+bool bql_trylock(void)
+{
+    g_assert(!bql_locked());
+    return qemu_mutex_trylock(&bql) == 0;
+}
+
 void bql_unlock(void)
 {
     g_assert(bql_locked());
